WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.618
Razor output encoding is an important
default protection when it is used correctly.

2
00:00:07.698 --> 00:00:13.011
Normal Razor output treats values as data
for the HTML context,

3
00:00:13.091 --> 00:00:20.409
which helps prevent user-controlled
content from changing the structure or behavior

4
00:00:20.489 --> 00:00:21.492
of the page.

5
00:00:21.572 --> 00:00:28.188
Developers should prefer those ordinary
encoded paths for displaying names,

6
00:00:28.268 --> 00:00:33.782
comments, labels, messages, and other
user-influenced values.

7
00:00:33.862 --> 00:00:38.775
Output encoding is not the same thing as
input validation.

8
00:00:38.855 --> 00:00:44.870
Validation decides whether a value is
acceptable for the application.

9
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:51.666
Encoding decides how the value is safely
represented in a destination context.

10
00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:57.461
A value can pass validation and still need
encoding before display.

11
00:00:57.541 --> 00:01:04.458
A value can also be rejected by validation
because it does not meet business rules.

12
00:01:04.538 --> 00:01:09.651
Raw HTML rendering should be rare,
justified, and reviewed.

13
00:01:09.731 --> 00:01:16.448
It may be appropriate for content that is
trusted, produced by the application,

14
00:01:16.528 --> 00:01:21.139
or passed through an approved sanitization
workflow.

15
00:01:21.219 --> 00:01:27.234
It should not become a shortcut for making
display problems disappear.

16
00:01:27.314 --> 00:01:32.627
Once a page treats text as markup, the
review burden increases.

17
00:01:32.707 --> 00:01:36.918
Different contexts require different
handling.

18
00:01:36.998 --> 00:01:43.514
A value placed into normal HTML body text
is not the same as a value placed into a

19
00:01:43.594 --> 00:01:47.905
JavaScript block, CSS, a URL, or an HTML
attribute.

20
00:01:47.985 --> 00:01:53.098
Razor and ASP.NET Core provide safer
patterns, tag helpers,

21
00:01:53.178 --> 00:02:00.195
and encoding behavior, but teams still
need to understand the context where data is

22
00:02:00.275 --> 00:02:01.178
inserted.

23
00:02:01.258 --> 00:02:08.075
Content Security Policy can provide
defense in depth for browser-facing pages.

24
00:02:08.155 --> 00:02:13.869
It can constrain some browser behavior
when configured carefully.

25
00:02:13.949 --> 00:02:18.661
It does not replace safe templating,
correct encoding,

26
00:02:18.741 --> 00:02:25.859
careful review of raw output, or a
disciplined approach to user-generated content.

27
00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:32.856
The practical rule is to keep untrusted
content on the encoded path unless there is

28
00:02:32.936 --> 00:02:37.547
a clear business reason and a reviewed
safety process.

29
00:02:37.627 --> 00:02:43.442
Razor Pages are easiest to review when
display behavior is ordinary,

30
00:02:43.522 --> 00:02:50.740
context-aware, and consistent across
layouts, partials, and page-specific views.
